WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:05.260
Want a preview of the battle between traditional
cars and self-driving ones? Don't look to

00:00:05.260 --> 00:00:12.300
Tesla, Apple, or Google. Look back a hundred
years, when automobiles took on horses — and

00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:15.120
the cars played dirty to win.

00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:21.560
"Ransom Olds, who founded the Olds Motor Works,
Oldsmobile, was particularly aggressive in

00:00:21.560 --> 00:00:25.130
his attacks on the horse. This was like a
psychologic thing —&nbsp;I don't know where

00:00:25.130 --> 00:00:28.300
it would fall in a diagnostic sense, but he
really hated horses."

00:00:28.300 --> 00:00:32.910
That's G. Wayne Miller —&nbsp;he wrote "Car
Crazy," a book that's about the really early

00:00:32.910 --> 00:00:39.480
days of cars. And early car marketers had
the horse on their mind. Those cars were unreliable

00:00:39.480 --> 00:00:45.190
and traveled on really bad roads, so carmakers
had a lot of work to do and a lot of gimmicks

00:00:45.190 --> 00:00:52.210
that they used. People were scared of cars;
one undertaker used to leave his card on automobile

00:00:52.210 --> 00:00:57.610
dashboards. So automakers had to be pretty
aggressive selling their invention. They raced

00:00:57.610 --> 00:01:02.800
cars around the country and manufacturers
insulted their competitors in ads.

00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:08.719
But some of their marketing firepower was
reserved for the horse. Of course, they invented

00:01:08.719 --> 00:01:13.789
the term horsepower, defining a car's power
by how many horses it could replace. But they

00:01:13.789 --> 00:01:19.780
also rigorously tested horse braking power
against their own cars, like a 15 horsepower

00:01:19.780 --> 00:01:25.530
De Dion against a team of horses. Yeah, the
machine stopped more quickly. And they taunted

00:01:25.530 --> 00:01:32.219
mercilessly. One Olds ad said, "Nature made
a mistake in giving the horse brain." The

00:01:32.219 --> 00:01:37.689
company claimed cars were cheaper and easier
than horses, even though most cars were really

00:01:37.689 --> 00:01:40.600
pricey and often required a home mechanic.

00:01:40.600 --> 00:01:46.590
Henry Ford hated horses too. "The horse is
doomed," Ford told a reporter. "These horses

00:01:46.590 --> 00:01:52.079
will be driven from the land, their troubles
will soon be over." Carmakers took on horses

00:01:52.079 --> 00:02:00.039
in public shame-races and in 1895 a new magazine
launched with a name that was not subtle:

00:02:00.039 --> 00:02:02.310
it was "The Horseless Age."

00:02:02.310 --> 00:02:08.190
On the road, cars often collided into horses,
and conflicts between car owners and farmers

00:02:08.190 --> 00:02:16.550
were common. But hating on horses wasn't just
personal. It was...logical.

00:02:16.550 --> 00:02:23.400
By the early 1900s, the laboring horse wasn't
noble — it was a public health hazard. Thousands

00:02:23.400 --> 00:02:30.140
of horses died each year from disease, overwork,
and old age, and those that lived produced

00:02:30.140 --> 00:02:32.950
millions of pounds of manure.

00:02:32.950 --> 00:02:36.170
"Those were significant and real public health
hazards."

00:02:36.170 --> 00:02:42.450
And urbanites were aware of it too. As cities
like New York grew really quickly, streets

00:02:42.450 --> 00:02:45.910
became jammed with horses and tons of horse
waste.

00:02:45.910 --> 00:02:52.650
"And there was another despicable downside:
not every horse owner was a kind person. And

00:02:52.650 --> 00:02:58.540
so horses were frequently whipped and beaten,
and when they died, you know, these despicable

00:02:58.540 --> 00:03:03.510
owners, often would leave a one ton, or ton
and a half, horse in the streets. Just leave

00:03:03.510 --> 00:03:06.450
it there. And that was a clear health hazard."

00:03:06.450 --> 00:03:11.440
Cars helped change that. Even Horseless Age
spoke up for the horse, saying in its first

00:03:11.440 --> 00:03:18.530
issue that cars would "spare the obedient
beast." So, yes, cars and horses did have

00:03:18.530 --> 00:03:25.590
a knock down, drag out fight, but there were...two
winners. And that's how it might be when self-driving

00:03:25.590 --> 00:03:32.320
cars come around: a fight to convince consumers,
followed by a likely decrease in fatalities

00:03:32.320 --> 00:03:32.780
and pollution.

00:03:32.780 --> 00:03:38.680
It might not always be easy. Motor World's
1905 headlines claimed that "since time of

00:03:38.680 --> 00:03:44.990
chariots" people have "opposed all new vehicles,"
and that might still be true. But, as Horseless

00:03:44.990 --> 00:03:50.570
Age put it more sweetly in their very first
issue, "Streets will be cleaner, jams and

00:03:50.570 --> 00:03:55.910
blockades less likely to occur, and accidents
less frequent."

00:04:02.900 --> 00:04:06.670
Thanks to G. Wayne Miller for talking to me,
this video is basically a chapter of his book

00:04:06.670 --> 00:04:10.970
and there are a lot of good stories in there.
I wanted to share one more insult, however,

00:04:10.970 --> 00:04:16.510
and it comes from the first issue of The Horseless
Age. This is what they said in response to

00:04:16.510 --> 00:04:23.090
the argument that the automobile scares horses:
"Suppose it does. So do locomotives, bicycles,

00:04:23.090 --> 00:04:27.600
street cars, Fourth of July celebrations,
and a dozen other things. Horses must get

00:04:27.600 --> 00:04:28.780
used to it."

00:04:28.780 --> 00:04:29.380
Boom.

